 "observations": [
  "The page number «187» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 73», in bold sans serif, underlined. At the foot, the signature «24*» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line. The titles (L01-L02), in bold, the sigla in roman: «Defectus lunares spatio longinquo (CN)», the lunar eclipses at the far distance, and O's «Elkusufet1) elkamarie2) libood elabaad (O)», al-kusūfāt al-qamariyya li-buʿd al-abʿad, the lunar eclipses for the greatest distance. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the titles in two lines, «C 83v, O 140r,» and «N 45v.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: fourteen columns divided by double vertical rules: «Motus3) latitudinis» over «Gr.», «Min.», «Gr.», «Min.»; «Digiti defectuum4)» over «Dig.», «Min.»; «Dakaicae5) casus6)» and «Dakaicae5) medietatis morae7)», each over «Min.», «Sec.»; and «Tabula8) proportionis» over «Partitiones diversitatum9)», two columns «Gr.», and «Dakaicae5) superationum10)», «Min.», «Sec.»; the units in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L03-L06); the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. The first ten columns end with the row of 0° (L29); the table of proportion continues to 60° (L36). A speck stands before the 0 of the fourth column in L29 and another in the empty column of the half mora at L31; not transcribed.",
  "L07-L29. The arguments of latitude, 10° 50′, 10° 30′ and then every half degree to 0° 0′, and their supplements, 169° 10′ to 180° 0′, with the reading of note a. The lunar eclipses at the greatest distance (Suter, pp. 86-88, JP2 0118-0120): the digits of the eclipse, the casus, from the first contact to the beginning of the totality or, in a partial eclipse, to the middle, and the half mora, half the totality, in minutes of the moon's path. Checked: the central row gives R + r = 29′ 16″ + 21′ 22″ = 50′ 38″ and R − r = 21′ 22″, the radius of the moon r = 14′ 38″, the value of Tab. 61 at the apogee (p. 175, JP2 0207), and the radius of the shadow R = 36′ 0″; the casus at 0° is the diameter of the moon, 29′ 16″. With the latitude β = 4° 30′ · sin x of Tab. 21 (p. 132, JP2 0164), 50′ 45″ at 10° 50′, larger than R + r, so that no eclipse occurs there, the casus with the half mora, √((R + r)² − β²), agrees within 20″ in the rows of total eclipse from 4° 0′ to 0°, and the half mora, √((R − r)² − β²), within 16″; at 4° 30′, the first row of total eclipse, the table has 41′ 4″ and 4′ 0″ for 43′ 12″ and 2′ 47″. The casus of the partial eclipses exceeds √((R + r)² − β²) by 2″ to 30″, and by 55″ at 9° 0′ (L11), 28′ 50″, where Tab. 74 (p. 188, JP2 0220) has O's 28′ 7″ and C the 50 of this page; at 8° 30′ and 5° 30′ (L12, L18) the printed 31′ 53″ and 43′ 56″ are 43″ and 25″ more than the computation, where Tab. 74 has 31′ 13″ and 43′ 36″: see the critical notes. At 1° 0′ the casus, 29′ 52″, 18″ more than the computation (29′ 34″), interrupts the decrease of the column, 30′ 3″, 29′ 52″, 29′ 19″, 29′ 16″.",
  "L07-L29, the digits. Checked: (R + r − β) · 12 : 2r gives 20 dig. 45.6′ at 0°, the 20 dig. 46′ of L29; in the other rows the table exceeds this by 2′ to 11′, by 6′ to 9′ from 8° 0′ to 2° 0′, except at 7° 0′ and 6° 30′ (L15-L16), 7 dig. 33′ and 8 dig. 40′, 17′ and 26′ more. After the first step, 40′, the digits grow by 54′ to 61′ for each half degree of the argument, by 56′ to 58′ from 6° 0′ on, except at these two rows, with the steps 68′, 67′ and 40′: see the notes on the readings. Suter's example (p. 87, JP2 0119, L04) takes from Tab. 74 for the argument 359° 12′ the values 19 dig. 16′, 29′ 39″ and 21′ 2″: they are those of this table at 0° 48′, interpolated between 0° 30′ and 1° 0′ (19 dig. 15.8′, 29′ 38.8″, 21′ 1.6″).",
  "L07-L36, the table of proportion: the arguments, the anomaly 2°-60° and 360° less it, 358°-300°, with the readings of notes d-g, and the sixtieths, «dakaicae superationum», by which the differences between the values at the far and at the near distance are multiplied (Suter, p. 88, JP2 0120, L22-L28). Checked: the values grow from 0′ 2″ at 2° to 14′ 0″ at 60°; they agree within 18″ with the share by which the distance of the moon on its epicycle of 5p 10′, √(60² + 5;10² + 2 · 60 · 5;10 · cos x), has fallen from its greatest value, 65p 10′, towards its least, 54p 50′, in sixtieths (14′ 4″ at 60°), being 8″ to 18″ larger from 14° to 30°; the fraction (1 − cos x) : 2 of the versed sine gives 15′ 0″ at 60°. The first values, 2″, 6″, 12″, 20″, 30″ and 42″ at 2°-12°, are n (n + 1) seconds for x = 2n.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: at 7° 0′ and 6° 30′ (notes b and c) C's minutes of the digits, 7 dig. 23′ and 8 dig. 21′, give the regular steps 58′, 58′ and 59′ from 6 dig. 25′ to 9 dig. 20′ (computed 7 dig. 16.1′ and 8 dig. 13.7′); O's 33 and 40, the edition's readings, give 68′, 67′ and 40′; Tab. 74 (p. 188) has C's 7 dig. 23′ and 8 dig. 21′ for the same distances from the node. The edition's readings are the arguments at 169° (note a, against N's 161) and 40°, 46°, 50° and 56° (notes d-g: C's 40, 46, 50 and 56 against O's 38, 47, 48 and 57). In the table of proportion the edition's 1′ 15″ at 16° (note h, against the 0′ of C and O) continues the column (0′ 57″, 1′ 15″, 1′ 34″; 1′ 4.3″ computed); O and N's 34 at 18° (note i, against C's 33) and O's 45 at 52° (note k, against C's 46) differ from C's by 1″, the computation giving 1′ 21.3″ and 10′ 46.2″.",
  "Notes 1-10: O's «Elkukufet» and «elkamene», the latter written correctly in the next table («recte in tab. sequent.»); «Motus» in C and O, «Argumentum» in the next table; O's «Azabaa ekucufet», al-aṣābiʿ al-kusūf, the digits of the eclipse; N's «Minuta» for «Dakaicae»; «elzukut» (O) and «elciicut» (C), al-suqūṭ, for «casus»; N's «dimidiae morae» and C's «nuzf elmukdh», niṣf al-mukth, half the mora; O's «Pagina» for «Tabula»; «Aguze elihtilef» (O) and «Ajuze elihtilef» (C), ajzāʾ al-ikhtilāf, the parts of the anomaly; «elfudhul» (C and O), al-fuḍūl, the excesses. Italics: «recte in tab. sequent.», «in tab. seq.», «om.». N04-N05: «supe-rationum» divided at the end of the line. N06: a faint speck after the C of note b; not transcribed."
 ],
